    <h2>What we check</h2>
    <p>A wellness exam is a conversation as much as an examination. While your pet sniffs around the room, we ask about appetite, thirst, energy, toilet habits and anything that has seemed different. Then, at their pace and often on the floor:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Eyes, ears, nose and mouth, including a proper look at every tooth.</li>
        <li>Heart and lungs, listened to in a quiet room.</li>
        <li>Belly, lymph nodes and skin, feeling for anything new.</li>
        <li>Joints and walk, especially in bigger and older dogs.</li>
        <li>Weight and body condition, with honest, kind advice on food.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Vaccines that fit your pet’s life</h2>
    <p>Every dog gets the core vaccines (distemper, parvovirus, adenovirus and rabies), and every cat the feline core (panleukopenia, herpesvirus, calicivirus and rabies). Beyond that we ask about your pet’s life: kennels, dog parks, hiking, hunting, outdoor cats. A city flat cat and a farm dog do not need the same list, and we will not sell you one.</p>
    <p class="callout">Rabbits need their own vaccines against myxomatosis and rabbit haemorrhagic disease (RHD). Our exotics vet sees them on the same visit.</p>
    <h2>After the visit</h2>
    <p>Before you leave you will have a short written summary: what we found, what we gave, what to watch, and when we would like to see you again. We email it too, so it is in your inbox before you get home.</p>
